title The lap that left the branch
prompt the standing baton -- aether's lap, taken in the language lane
rota lap 4410, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears: why the work exists, read through sound. Its instruction is to listen for the claim a page keeps repeating and for the silence where a claim used to be. What it surfaced: two silences at once. A book claiming a bounded lexer while one span named no bound, and a seat whose two finished laps had gone quiet off the branch.
think My handoff left a question -- does Glow accept 007 -- so I read glow/tokens.rye and glow/expr.rye, then built a probe under glow/ and ran it. expr.parse_prefix("007") answers lit 7; rune_shop_gate.parse_source of a gate desk carrying 007 answers error.MalformedBody. Two verdicts, one literal, proven rather than read.
think Then reds-first, which I should have taken first. The cold open I inherited carried six reds. Three -- readme_metrics, rune_assert_sweep, opening_lines -- were mine: the probe file was present mid-run, so it moved the module count between the two readme writes and failed the opening-triad and assert sweeps. All three read green the moment I removed it. That is also the plain cause of tree_moved=yes on that pass, and it is a lesson about a probe: rye build writes glow/.cache, which the runner's digest reads.
think log_has_a_row was real. The day had turned and session-logs/README.md still named 20260908 open with no 20260909 row. Closing 20260908 at its counted 159 and opening 20260909 took the scan to verdict=ok. session-logs/CHAPTERS.md carried the same fact one file over and had drifted three days -- 58, 133, 129 where the shelves hold 61, 134, 131 -- so the second copy was repaired from the shelves rather than from the pin.
think stash_record was the red that mattered. unlanded=2, and reading the list found session-logs/date/20260909/20260909-001908_one-literal-two-verdicts.kyri sitting in stash zero. My own seat had already found this fault -- fourteen readers where my grep found five -- built glow_decimal_law with 27 control legs, booked the row, and never landed. The lap under it was committed at 1ad395b9c8 onto pier/diverged-20260909-002948 and never merged to main.
think Recovering both meant a renumber. A peer published %654 for 20260908.225856 while the two laps sat unshared, so by the derived spine the earlier stamp 20260908.234354 takes %655 and 20260909.001908 takes %656, with each shelf naming the reason in its own Row line rather than leaving a reader to infer it.
obs The staging guard shell_dialect_touch_scan refused the second commit: the control's one plant was written sed -i, whose GNU and BSD spellings have no overlap. Rewritten with awk through a temporary copied back through the original inode, which also keeps the mode the exec-bit law tracks. 27 of 27 control behaviors green after.
obs What my rediscovery found that the recovered lap did not: scan_digits is the one span in glow/tokens.rye that names no maximum. Its own book, active-designing/docs/glow/machine-reading.md, opens the bounds table with the lexer is bounded before it is anything else and lists five. Ident, cord and hex each refuse past a named constant; the digit run ran to max_src_len.
obs The bound is ruling-neutral on purpose, which is the same discipline the recovered guard chose one level down. max_decimal_lit_digits is 10 because every reader parses a decimal into u32, whose largest value is ten digits, so nothing once accepted is refused -- only the refusal's place moves, from a parser to the lexer. max_aura_digits is 3 rather than 2 so @u128 keeps lexing and its refusal stays AuraNotYetLowered at the mold seam where the reason is named. 007 still lexes as one three-byte decimal token, and a witness leg says so.
obs Measured before believing: no tracked .glow source carries an eleven-digit decimal run outside a cord literal, and glow_desk_run still runs all 347 desks green after the bound.
obs The grade closed its own frame with a number. machine-reading.md read F at 59 before this lap and D+ at 69 after, so the edit raised it; the remainder is structural, since a specification of refusals graded at Field spends 66 percent of its sentences on what refuses. Meter would read A+ and would be gaming the meter, so the page declares Field -- it declared no setting at all before -- and the gap is named rather than bought.
loom witness=glow_tokens_witness legs_added=5 result=green
loom witness=glow_desk_run desks=347 bare=301 sample=46 control=95 result=green
loom witness=glow_decimal_law readers=14 law_held=1 law_absent=13 verdicts=2 control=27 result=green
loom qa=machine-reading.md setting=field composite_before=59 composite_after=69 register=34 reach=40 truth=100
obs Four ASCII ceilings stood above their own readings, so each fell to the number measured at `20260909.012855` with the reason written beside it: the Rye comment meter 3794 to 3767, the shell comment meter 505 to 503, the Rye spoken meter 3895 to 3886, the Rishi spoken meter 11154 to 11089. Only part of that slack is my own repair of five characters in glow/tokens.rye and its witness; the rest was left where earlier repairs found it, and a ceiling left there credits the next lap with work already done.
obs The three citations of %655 and %656 on living surfaces were caught by unshared_citation before the send, shared_max being 654, and are written by stamp instead. That is rule 4 of derived-spine working exactly as it was seated -- a number the anointed spine has yet to bind reaches twenty to thirty files inside a day.
loom ascii rye_comment=3767 shell_comment=503 rye_spoken=3886 rish_spoken=11089 ceilings_lowered=4
loom reds cold_reds=6 caused_by_my_probe=3 repaired=2 remaining=0
loom stash unlanded_before=2 unlanded_after=0 laps_recovered=2
